  "state": {
    "case_id": "10",
    "case_summary": "An 82-year-old man was admitted to the hospital presenting with melaena and dizziness upon standing. His medical history included systemic sclerosis diagnosed three years prior and pulmonary fibrosis. He was on prednisolone and azathioprine. There was no history of liver disease or alcohol abuse. On physical examination, his blood pressure was 95/65 mmHg, and his pulse was 90 beats per minute. Bilateral fine inspiratory crackles were noted. The abdomen was soft and non-tender. Rectal examination confirmed melaena. Laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 12,700/mm3, haemoglobin level of 10.9 g/dl, platelet count of 186,000/mm3, and mean corpuscular volume of 99.4 fl. Blood urea was 12.5 mmol/l, creatinine 68 μmol/l, with normal electrolytes and glucose at 6.3 mmol/l. Liver function tests revealed serum alanine aminotransferase at 12 u/l, bilirubin at 12 μmol/l, γ-glutamyl transferase at 54 u/l, alkaline phosphatase at 35 u/l, total protein at 52 g/l, albumin at 27 g/l, and C-reactive protein at 12. Coagulation studies were within normal limits. Factor V Leiden mutation was absent. Hepatitis viral serology and autoantibody screen were negative, and α-1-antitrypsin levels were normal. Ferritin was 103 μmol/l, caeruloplasmin was 0.25 μmol/l, and α-fetoprotein was 0.9 kU/L. Urgent gastroscopy identified oesophageal varices, fresh blood, and clots in the stomach. During the procedure, the oesophageal varices began to bleed. Haemostasis was not achieved with sclerotherapy, necessitating the insertion of a Sengstaken tube. The patient was admitted to the High Dependency Unit, started on intravenous glypressin and antibiotics, and received a transfusion of four units of blood. A repeat gastroscopy the following day showed two columns of grade II oesophageal varices, two oesophageal ulcers likely secondary to sclerotherapy, and mild portal gastropathy.\n\nThere was no evidence of any excess inflammation and no features to suggest cirrhosis or drug-induced liver disease. Overall this was essentially normal liver tissue. Ultrasound scan of the abdomen revealed a small liver with coarse texture and no focal lesion. Hepato-portal flow was demonstrated in the portal vein. The common bile duct was of normal caliber. The spleen was enlarged (bipolar length 12.8 cm). A moderate amount of free peritoneal fluid was noted. CT scan confirmed the absence of portal vein thrombosis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
